{"voice":"en-US-JennyNeural","words":{"restless":"76116662fa101026","routine":"012da8a947ea870d","rhythm":"da06f0d7b6a05032","deliberate":"3c513544bca249e4","cognition":"e2d60412038d6476","persuade":"cd0de7c91effbd6d","absorb":"846e2a86e5a9b6ea","hesitate":"81cbe4e688fba79b","distract":"efed43f00fc8ef2f","insight":"f67b30498047f2f3","generate":"9e59d42533de1328","efficient":"8527edb7b0cd63a6","conduct":"1f005da3de016bc8","dissipate":"e95f494c23646c55","throttle":"fb224d511f309e99","compromise":"faaabf119c6922ad","durable":"6802cb5d70b48e54","graphics":"85bf98024248834c","sensor":"2fc03be5f18a42c3","thermal":"4d511848d7291c64","biologist":"885d121cbe7a966e","creature":"93fda888e2185c78","microscope":"34a95f2c94b444bd","survive":"8dd6d34decdc0d26","metabolism":"671e483513b16aae","extreme":"6928e84932543506","strategy":"31e10e63573191fe","adapt":"40fe84e6878b876f","preserve":"e900984dc91dc9c7","exaggerate":"1461b27b6a26a96a","hypothesis":"ed4b436c75459c85","variable":"b46d0172433dd689","precise":"d53a4c62525d45af","isolate":"eb5779e2b2ea1515","symptom":"3e15bf38ad06bd1c","frustration":"5697cf88c93b2e2a","evidence":"100ec4462dec34f6","diagnosis":"fe134dc58ea9b9a8","revise":"a5062f56eb9c6341","assumption":"577633048497efd1","algae":"52169aa4ea278b85","partnership":"ac6e5d1834a1af14","photosynthesis":"f73fecc9ff6b5146","expel":"857cb65d0ffb77d7","bleach":"ea1761e93aa5f286","vulnerable":"36e83e3080c38d7d","symbiosis":"1e8dd01eae97280a","monitor":"9796809f7dae482d","habitat":"1da0e9a6c6b0d50d","emission":"b5da25e0cd1def45","lithium":"c50045767c6c8fa1","ion":"bbb286479461a1d5","electrode":"64f086124818ab37","efficiency":"fff33dfb39d30f60","density":"bbbf26a1fbb280a5","rechargeable":"a9c3870e3a0793f5","capacity":"7cb1f56d3fbe09e8","recycle":"089f4e36079ef274","alternative":"816d63e63c53fbbc","property":"269fe3733d17108a","constellation":"7af5d7d68eb54ff0","receiver":"8029e7aeed18f846","calculate":"a8f700e7c0a3e16c","orbit":"db4baacb8d20becf","gravity":"ada87b6cd2a041a3","accurate":"72c8f75b62572f59","synchronize":"d8da2c7c4289c69a","accumulate":"da612b00046cfa57","fragment":"5172cf7b97e10171","detect":"2b66455f7e2e8041","precautionary":"5b7e25593572b1f6","polymer":"457f0e67b035e063","mechanical":"c2b37ef88f80018b","complex":"6ff16b36dc77810e","additive":"62d123062a8c4859","indifference":"16a2c503a048e57d","consequence":"f4475dac731e939f"},"sentences":{"After scrolling, she still felt restless.":"d169c19044b87a4c","A calm morning routine helps on exam days.":"1e929ca060e3fa43","The rhythm of walking helps some people think.":"8883422c0b7216e4","She made a deliberate choice to pocket the phone.":"2a24c2d45f80a4af","Cognition includes attention, memory and reasoning.":"ad6378ab773347ea","Do not let ads persuade you every minute must be online.":"0b7b7299f53c4217","A short walk helped him absorb the passage.":"59fd44f373fcfd3d","If you hesitate before studying, start small.":"90d272f4440ade26","Notifications distract you from deep work.":"b73ec221889891b2","Quiet walks can bring unexpected insight.":"3ba3c47e893050f2","The chip generates heat when it works hard.":"ec85fcddd76f4828","An efficient cooler removes heat faster.":"1a18c8151b85a7d5","Metal can conduct heat away from the chip.":"c3f5315222d44169","The case helps dissipate heat into the air.":"5b36b6705404a9d7","Phones throttle the chip when too hot.":"32868ca36b98b888","Speed and safety need a compromise.":"9f8ee8ba70920833","A durable design protects the battery.":"5f0891c0b983b7ad","Game graphics push the chip hard.":"4b7f8f361e6e4db5","The phone watches heat with sensors.":"20c1ee631e36db4f","Thermal energy moves through metal layers.":"85c72841256a4973","A biologist studies living systems.":"91aff60804a09923","A tiny creature lived in the moss.":"41aed6929520f489","Under a microscope the water bear looks chubby.":"73cca3e3aceec388","Some species can survive extreme dryness.":"3f5804e83434814f","Metabolism slows almost to a stop.":"b0d1022d718fe4d8","Extreme cold can freeze ordinary cells.":"be1027d8556e8dcc","Waiting can be a survival strategy.":"8fd828c3fb1c41bd","Species adapt when environments change.":"c5fb8b75aa53b3cc","Research may help preserve medicines.":"b5c401e948e218a2","Headlines often exaggerate early lab results.":"cf3712200320e42e","State a hypothesis before changing code.":"9e155150d1e57cdd","Change only one variable at a time.":"ebcc0b8abfd8ce0c","A precise description narrows the search.":"c65693c7d43394fe","Isolate the buggy function with a tiny test.":"1990671390bc1ac1","Describe the symptom before guessing causes.":"869bca736bcdf164","Frustration leads to random clicking.":"8bd9140eb9300d81","Bring evidence, not only opinions.":"61ac5f279ade4413","Evidence turns debate into diagnosis.":"3e00e810035ac5da","Test, then revise your hypothesis.":"bed7f295f9dcf200","Note the wrong assumption you held.":"d307f4a4494503e3","Tiny algae live inside coral tissues.":"95d91163cd8269de","The partnership provides food and shelter.":"8c74b372595c2496","Algae make food by photosynthesis.":"06af0d63e55e9f4d","Stressed corals expel their algae.":"74bb9e6cdf7c05a9","Heat can bleach a reef.":"5e57785dd7e1e4e4","Bleached coral is more vulnerable to disease.":"c447d6c68fa9380b","Symbiosis links coral and algae.":"317de0632d31a5a6","Scientists monitor sea temperatures.":"da64fa9eaa05a631","Fish lose habitat when reefs collapse.":"9ceb60dd48d24db9","Cut greenhouse gas emissions.":"e6a8a96e644387ce","Lithium ions move inside the battery.":"b5568d5f6ad6f268","Lithium ions shuttle between electrodes.":"17fc02615b653b75","Ions move between electrodes.":"5b3d9448b7a6f2b2","Cells store energy with useful efficiency.":"0f61c663ca84642e","Energy density matters for phones and cars.":"1059b828b876071a","Rechargeable cells changed portable electronics.":"d020d9a234f389fa","Battery capacity fades after many cycles.":"1ae794e191ef8522","We need to recycle old packs better.":"ec263df45454e17d","Engineers look for safer alternatives.":"4079e649ea789f35","Lithium's properties fit a demanding job.":"dafaf810cd4835a5","A constellation of satellites carries clocks.":"f46b71967c6feb19","A receiver on Earth compares timing signals.":"1ed8fde0545bcdac","Receivers calculate position from signals.":"8af2715ba6f99cc4","Clocks in orbit need corrections.":"1e54dad08ac30a43","Weaker gravity affects clocks.":"fb9d9591312957be","Corrections keep navigation accurate.":"a34f4525b3f6e965","Ask what assumptions hide inside a tool.":"08411e36ab11d6d9","A map needs synchronized time.":"55a027ab301a8d8f","Errors would accumulate without corrections.":"72439c0e3cca9b77","A century-old insight about time.":"b23493be7e1975ed","Plastics fragment into tiny pieces.":"4eb21d1a0be81bd6","Labs detect particles in water.":"657ebf9d679623f8","A precautionary question is still fair.":"fc5f1660dadd95ca","Polymers are long-chain molecules.":"bc29d0fc0621379e","Mechanical stress can break chains.":"9d3c3a59fb1ef842","The pathways are complex.":"63fee51423fadf76","Chemical additives may affect risk.":"54dc10b0d5e2f166","Avoid both panic and indifference.":"324f1720283265d5","Prefer durable goods when possible.":"248cf53b60ea0ef3","Convenience came with consequence.":"776ef0a083ee8fb0"},"passages":{"The Quiet Superpower of Walking":"780da6ea2559f5c8","After the last bell, many students reach for their phones before they reach for the bus door. A short video promises a quick laugh. Then another. Ten minutes disappear, and the mind still feels restless.":"726bdee20e5802ce","Try a different routine. Put the phone away and walk for those same minutes. You do not need a park. A noisy street is enough. What matters is the rhythm of your steps and a small, deliberate choice to protect attention.":"da83430d7eb16892","Walking looks simple, but it quietly trains focus. When you scroll, your eyes jump every few seconds. When you walk, your body settles into a steady beat. A stuck math idea, a half-written sentence, even a worry you tried to ignore—these thoughts sometimes return with a clearer shape.":"a086d06d5a9cd9e7","Researchers who study cognition often describe this as a gentle shift of mental resources: less chasing of new stimuli, more space for connections already forming. You will not invent a new theorem on every walk. You may, however, notice which problem still bothers you—and that notice is useful.":"a917556ed72827c3","Some friends may persuade you to stay online. You can still protect a short walk. Say you will join the chat in ten minutes. Most people accept a clear boundary more easily than a soft “maybe later.”":"1ef0602520e00dda","A walk is a deliberate pause, not an empty gap. It will not replace sleep or honest effort in class. But over a week, quiet walks add up. You may find it easier to absorb a reading passage at night, because the brain arrives warmer and less distracted.":"374e97d3da29bebd","If you hesitate before opening a thick vocabulary list, walk first. After you return, study for just fifteen minutes. Treat the walk as a tiny experiment: note whether ideas feel clearer. That quiet space is often where insight begins—because the superpower does not shout; it simply gives attention a chance to return.":"b82bf75e15832434","Why Your Phone Feels Like a Tiny Stove":"a271815860a52a3e","After a long gaming session, your phone feels like a tiny stove. That heat is not a mystery. Your chip is working hard, and hard work generates energy you can feel through the case.":"d6994dca73e162fd","Inside the phone, billions of tiny switches flip on and off. Each flip uses a little electricity. When many flips happen at once—for graphics, sound, and network—the chip becomes less efficient at staying cool. Extra energy that cannot stay as useful work turns into heat.":"2b168792191bce01","Materials try to conduct heat away from the chip toward the case. Metal layers and graphite sheets act as quiet highways for thermal energy. If the case cannot dissipate heat fast enough into the air, temperature climbs. Then the phone may throttle itself: it slows the chip on purpose to protect the battery and the screen.":"ea3b25da369d82c7","That is why a heavy game can feel smooth at first and sticky later. The device is making a compromise between speed and safety. Fans in laptops move air; phones mostly rely on quiet metal, clever software, and the simple fact that hotter surfaces radiate and transfer heat more quickly—until humans complain that the pocket is too warm.":"de9c82dbd91c1b85","For science-track students, the lesson is practical. Heat is energy taking another form. If you charge while gaming, you add another heat source. Giving the phone a rest, removing a thick case, or lowering brightness can help more than shouting at the screen.":"6a72fc6a1c161c72","Engineers balance performance and temperature every day. Software watches sensors, shifts tasks, and sometimes dims the display. Next time your phone warms up, you are holding a small physics demo—and a durable design that chooses to slow down rather than burn out.":"f585118312f2fc32","Understanding the trade-off makes the “sticky” moment less mysterious. Heat is not angry electricity; it is work changing form, and software choosing safety over speed when the case cannot keep up. That choice is engineering, not failure—and it is the same kind of compromise you meet in lab reports: control one variable, protect the system, then measure again.":"849ec55555178595","Earth's Quiet Tough Guys":"492ef24779f58036","If you imagine the toughest animal on Earth, you might picture a shark or a polar bear. Biologists often point somewhere smaller: a tardigrade, a creature so tiny that it looks like a walking water bear under a microscope.":"2e18824641e560a6","Tardigrades live in moss, soil, and the thin films of water on leaves. When conditions turn harsh, many species do not simply die. They enter a state that looks almost like death—shutting down metabolism so deeply that they can survive extreme dryness, freezing temperatures, and even the vacuum of space in carefully designed experiments.":"f774b8e389630795","The key idea is not magic armor. It is a survival strategy. Some proteins help protect cell structures; water is replaced in ways that prevent ice crystals from tearing membranes. When moisture returns, the animal can revive and continue its quiet life.":"befc0dc5f385e914","For high-school readers who like biology, tardigrades are a reminder that “adapt” is more than a textbook verb. Adaptation can mean changing what a body does when the environment refuses to stay friendly. It can also mean waiting—pausing life processes until the world becomes livable again.":"2c194f91b4281827","Scientists study these creatures because extreme survival raises useful questions. How do cells keep DNA from breaking under stress? Which molecules act like shields? Answers may someday help preserve medicines, crops, or even human tissues, though popular headlines often exaggerate how close we are to “immortal astronauts.”":"db9120170d1e0376","Still, the lesson for a curious student is clear. Nature’s toughest solutions are often microscopic. Before you dismiss a puddle of moss as boring, remember that it may hide an animal that has practiced patience for hundreds of millions of years—and that patience, in biology, can look like strength.":"5f95837408c7129a","When you next open a biology diagram of cells and membranes, ask a harder question: what happens when the diagram’s polite conditions disappear? Tardigrades answer with a shrug of chemistry and time. They do not conquer the desert; they wait it out, then walk again.":"bc3fafa6082afa50","Debug Like a Lab Report":"68a21049d167c529","When a program fails, beginners often change three things at once and hope the error disappears. Scientists would call that a messy experiment. Debugging works better when you treat it like a lab report: state a hypothesis, change one variable, and check the result.":"79a9c777226b8028","Start by describing the symptom clearly. Does the program crash, or does it run and give the wrong answer? A precise description already narrows the search. Next, form a hypothesis: “Maybe the loop stops one step too early.” Then test that idea without rewriting the whole file.":"c630ceaaf7a4be53","Good debuggers isolate the problem. Comment out a section, print a middle value, or run a tiny example with known input and expected output. If the tiny example fails, the bug is nearby. If it succeeds, look elsewhere. This is the same logic you use when a chemistry reaction refuses to turn blue: check measurements before blaming the textbook.":"290689485028e535","Emotion is part of the process. Frustration makes people click randomly. A short pause—stretch, breathe, write one sentence about what you already know—often restores calm. Calm minds notice patterns; panicked minds generate noise.":"142c6d0baa364a57","In school projects, teammates sometimes argue about style before they agree on facts. Bring evidence to the table: a screenshot, a failing test, a line number. Evidence turns debate into diagnosis. It also trains a habit that exams reward—clear reasoning under pressure.":"6cc187ecf3dd9e8e","The deeper lesson is transferable. Whether you are fixing code, a physics formula, or a history timeline, the method stays familiar: observe, hypothesize, test, revise. Debugging is not a special talent reserved for programmers. It is scientific thinking wearing headphones.":"d8d29ec47565668f","When your program finally works, do not delete the trail too fast. Note what wrong assumption you held. That note is a gift to future-you—and it is exactly the kind of reflective sentence teachers love in lab notebooks.":"4fb48f53f214d7d5","When Color Leaves the Reef":"2113f9cd73a58785","Coral reefs look like underwater cities built from stone and color. The color, however, is not only rock. It comes largely from tiny algae living inside coral tissues. In a healthy partnership, the algae share food made by photosynthesis, and the coral provides shelter.":"ca7ef98d534fcbc9","When ocean temperatures rise for too long, the partnership breaks down. Stressed corals expel their algae partners. Without the algae, the white skeleton shows through—a process reporters call bleaching. Bleaching does not always mean instant death, but it means the coral is running on empty and more vulnerable to disease.":"243194073bb26cf8","Why should a high-school student in a coastal Chinese city care? Because reefs protect shorelines, support fisheries, and store lessons about symbiosis—the idea that two different organisms can share a fate. When heat waves become more frequent, whole neighborhoods of reef can tip from colorful to pale within weeks.":"f6b47498111dfbf7","Local clubs, museums, and school science fairs can turn bleaching from a distant headline into a measurable question: what temperature histories sit above the reefs we still have, and which human choices move those numbers?":"0eca930571b12cb5","Scientists monitor sea-surface temperatures and warn of bleaching risk the way weather apps warn of storms. The language is careful: risk is not destiny. Some reefs recover if waters cool and water quality stays decent. Others collapse into rubble fields where fish lose habitat and local economies lose a living museum.":"015ce383f5eb7a74","Solutions sound familiar from other climate topics: cut greenhouse gas emissions, reduce local pollution, and protect remaining healthy reefs as “seed banks” for recovery. None of these is a single homework worksheet. All of them reward the same reading skill you practice here—connecting a vivid scene to a larger system.":"1b3e47dbc61f9768","Next time you see a postcard of blue water and bright coral, read it as a temporary balance. Color on a reef is a contract between heat, light, and living partners. When the contract fails, the ocean does not shout. It simply turns pale—and asks whether humans noticed in time.":"7cedaa311257501e","Why Lithium Rules the Pocket":"d43c69982f529ca0","Open your backpack and you will likely find a quiet metal worker: lithium. Inside phone batteries and many electric vehicles, lithium ions shuttle between electrodes, storing and releasing energy with impressive efficiency for their weight.":"880a3c85d445b039","Chemists care about energy density—how much usable energy you can pack into a kilogram or a liter. Lithium is light, and its ions move in ways that make rechargeable cells practical. That is why a slim phone can last a day, and why bus companies talk about ranges in hundreds of kilometers rather than in single-digit dreams.":"af8d3cfc44011627","Nothing comes free. Mining and processing raise environmental and social questions. Batteries age: capacity fades after many charge cycles. Heat, deep discharge, and cheap chargers can shorten life. Recycling is improving, but collecting old packs still lags behind the speed of new purchases.":"98b942877109c498","Textbook redox half-equations become less abstract when you picture ions as commuters with tickets called voltage. The ticket price—the cell potential—helps explain why some chemistries win in phones while others stay in labs.":"ee37cd5df4f323d9","For a science-track student, lithium is a bridge between classroom electrochemistry and daily life. Oxidation and reduction are not only exam diagrams; they are the invisible commute of ions that keep your map app alive on the way home. Understanding trade-offs—lighter phones versus harder recycling—is part of reading modern technology honestly.":"107b6297bef7dbc3","Engineers look for alternatives and improvements: safer electrolytes, longer cycle life, better thermal management. Policy makers argue about charging networks and second-life batteries in storage systems. Consumers make smaller choices: avoid extreme heat, use certified chargers, and do not treat a swollen battery as a fashion accessory.":"0aa4cf074391a38e","The next time your power bank saves a late-night study session, remember the periodic table sitting in your pocket. Lithium rules not because it is magical, but because its properties fit a demanding job description: light enough to carry, reactive enough to work, and manageable enough—if we treat waste and safety as seriously as we treat screen brightness.":"cb64cb9c1ce97eb6","Clocks That Need Einstein":"b283bf1808315f32","Your map app feels ordinary until it fails in a tunnel. Behind the blue dot is a constellation of satellites carrying atomic clocks. Receivers on Earth compare timing signals and calculate position. Tiny errors in time become large errors in distance, because light—and radio signals—travel so fast that a billionth of a second already maps to roughly thirty centimeters.":"ef7b1b01e07b5deb","Here is the surprise that delights physics teachers: without corrections from relativity, GPS would drift unacceptably. Clocks in orbit experience both special-relativistic effects from their speed and general-relativistic effects from weaker gravity. Engineers do not debate philosophy in the control room; they apply formulas so that the system remains accurate for navigation, aviation, and surveying.":"46cdbd82ed73a2fa","You do not need to derive Einstein’s field equations to use a phone. You do need the habit of asking what assumptions hide inside a “simple” tool. A map is not only geography; it is synchronized time distributed across space. When someone says science is useless outside exams, GPS is a polite counterexample in your pocket.":"053ac6ab78c514a2","For exam readers, the passage trains a familiar skill: follow a chain from phenomenon to principle to consequence. Phenomenon: precise positioning. Principle: time and gravity couple to measurement. Consequence: technology must include corrections or accuracy collapses. That chain mirrors how good reading items are built—and how lab reasoning is graded.":"e63644e533347db7","Critics sometimes claim relativity is “only theory.” Operational systems answer differently: if the corrections were omitted, errors would accumulate far beyond what drivers and pilots can ignore. Theory earns trust when predictions survive daily use at planetary scale.":"1db916d54af0a2c2","So the next time the blue dot snaps to the right street, thank an invisible chorus of clocks—and a century-old insight that time is not a background drumbeat the same for everyone. Navigation works because physics refused to stay on the chalkboard.":"34cc2bf3890477be","The Invisible Dust We Drink":"0a86f48a2a255207","Plastic bottles are easy to see. Microplastics are not. They are fragments smaller than five millimeters—some far smaller—born when larger plastics break down, when clothes shed fibers in the wash, or when industry makes tiny beads on purpose.":"8d117e51b36c595a","Researchers have found these particles in rivers, seafood, table salt, and even air. The scientific conversation is careful: detecting particles is not the same as proving exact harm in every case. Still, the precautionary question is fair. If materials designed to last for decades keep fragmenting, where do the pieces go, and what do living systems do with them?":"932e52e0d68174a8","Chemistry class already gave you part of the vocabulary. Polymers are long-chain molecules; sunlight, heat, and mechanical stress can snap those chains into smaller bits. Filters catch some fibers; many escape. Wastewater plants were not originally designed as plastic nets.":"c652c9fd5b898e1a","For students who like clear causes and effects, microplastics are frustratingly complex. Sources are many; pathways tangle; effects may differ by particle size, shape, and chemical additives. That complexity is why good science writing avoids panic headlines and still refuses indifference.":"676f1d5029a7574b","Practical responses exist at different scales. Designers can reduce unnecessary single-use packaging. Cities can improve waste sorting and stormwater control. Labs develop better detection methods. Individuals can prefer durable goods and wash bags that catch fibers—small acts that do not “solve the ocean” alone, but train the habit of treating materials as systems, not as disappearing tricks.":"2815a0f2be5b7085","Exam-style reading often asks what a writer implies rather than what a slogan shouts. Here the implication is steady: invisible materials still belong to a chain of production, use, and disposal—and chains can be redesigned.":"6b4bc7efc564be76","Reading about invisible dust is also reading about responsibility. The plastic age delivered convenience. It also delivered fragments that outlast the afternoon snack they once wrapped. Between convenience and consequence sits the same skill this shelf practices: hold a fact, follow a chain, and decide what deserves your next careful question.":"91dc399aaaaefd07"},"wordsLower":{"restless":"76116662fa101026","routine":"012da8a947ea870d","rhythm":"da06f0d7b6a05032","deliberate":"3c513544bca249e4","cognition":"e2d60412038d6476","persuade":"cd0de7c91effbd6d","absorb":"846e2a86e5a9b6ea","hesitate":"81cbe4e688fba79b","distract":"efed43f00fc8ef2f","insight":"f67b30498047f2f3","generate":"9e59d42533de1328","efficient":"8527edb7b0cd63a6","conduct":"1f005da3de016bc8","dissipate":"e95f494c23646c55","throttle":"fb224d511f309e99","compromise":"faaabf119c6922ad","durable":"6802cb5d70b48e54","graphics":"85bf98024248834c","sensor":"2fc03be5f18a42c3","thermal":"4d511848d7291c64","biologist":"885d121cbe7a966e","creature":"93fda888e2185c78","microscope":"34a95f2c94b444bd","survive":"8dd6d34decdc0d26","metabolism":"671e483513b16aae","extreme":"6928e84932543506","strategy":"31e10e63573191fe","adapt":"40fe84e6878b876f","preserve":"e900984dc91dc9c7","exaggerate":"1461b27b6a26a96a","hypothesis":"ed4b436c75459c85","variable":"b46d0172433dd689","precise":"d53a4c62525d45af","isolate":"eb5779e2b2ea1515","symptom":"3e15bf38ad06bd1c","frustration":"5697cf88c93b2e2a","evidence":"100ec4462dec34f6","diagnosis":"fe134dc58ea9b9a8","revise":"a5062f56eb9c6341","assumption":"577633048497efd1","